# Schemas

A schema from `howdy/openapi/schema` describes a JSON value once. From that
one description you get a decoder, an encoder, and the JSON Schema an
OpenAPI document shows.

## Objects

Objects are built the way `gleam/dynamic/decode` decoders are, with one more
argument per field: a function that reads the field back out of the value.
That function is what lets the same schema encode.

```gleam
import howdy/openapi/schema.{type Schema}

pub type User {
  User(id: Int, name: String, nickname: Option(String))
}

pub fn user() -> Schema(User) {
  {
    use id <- schema.field("id", schema.int(), fn(user: User) { user.id })
    use name <- schema.field("name", schema.string(), fn(user: User) {
      user.name
    })
    use nickname <- schema.optional_field(
      "nickname",
      schema.string(),
      fn(user: User) { user.nickname },
    )
    schema.success(User(id:, name:, nickname:))
  }
  |> schema.named("User")
}
```

`optional_field` decodes a missing field or `null` as `None`, and leaves
`None` out when encoding.

`schema.named` shows the schema once in the document, under
`components/schemas/User`, and refers to it everywhere else. Two different
schemas with the same name make building the document panic.

To document an object, the fields after each one are found by running the
rest of the chain with placeholder values. Keep the chain to `field` calls
and a final `success`: if it branches on a field's value, only the branch
the placeholder takes is documented.

## Types

- `string()`, `int()`, `float()` and `bool()`. `float` also accepts
  integers such as `5`.
- `list(of:)` and `dict(of:)`, a JSON object used as a map with string keys.
- `nullable(schema)`, a value or `null`, as an `Option`.
- `enum([Admin, Member], name)`, a string standing for one of your values,
  where `name` gives each variant its string; written as a `case` it cannot
  miss one.
- `one_of([variant(card_schema, to: Card), variant(bank_schema, to: Bank)],
  encode:)`, a value that is one of several shapes, decoded by the first
  variant that matches and shown as `anyOf`. `encode` is your `fn(a) -> Json`,
  usually a `case` calling `schema.to_json` with the matching inner schema.
- `map(schema, to:, from:)` changes the type, such as wrapping an `Int` in a
  `UserId`. `try_map` is for changes that can fail, such as parsing a date,
  and fails with your message.

To use a schema outside an endpoint, `schema.to_json(value, schema)` encodes
and `schema.decoder(schema)` gives a `gleam/dynamic/decode` decoder.

## Constraints

Constraints are checked while decoding, and shown in the document as the
matching JSON Schema keyword. Their messages are the ones `howdy/validate`
uses:

- `min_length`, `max_length` and `not_empty` for strings.
- `email`, the light check from `validate.email`, shown as `format: email`.
- `minimum` and `maximum` for integers; `float_minimum` and `float_maximum`
  for floats.
- `min_items` and `max_items` for lists.

```gleam
schema.string()
|> schema.rule(validate.trim())
|> schema.not_empty
|> schema.max_length(50)
```

`schema.rule` runs any `howdy/validate` rule. Rules can change the value, as
`validate.trim()` does, so later constraints see the trimmed text. The
document cannot show what a rule checks, so add a `description` when
clients need to know.

## Documentation

- `description(schema, text)`.
- `example(schema, value)`, encoded with the schema itself.
- `format(schema, name)`, such as `"date-time"` or `"uuid"`. This only
  documents the value; pair it with `try_map` or `rule` to check it.
- `deprecated(schema)`.
